assets/diagrams/vipi-ocr-recovery.svg:
How VIPI recovers text that OCR missed
VIPI first reads the original image with OCR and checks the result for quality signals such as empty text, low confidence, small text, unusual symbols, low contrast or rare words. Without a signal, the visual review and the 2x upscale are skipped. With a signal, a visual decision model answers whether text is missing, whether the image detail is enough and which fix is supported; a rotation or contrast fix is kept only if it improves the reading. A signaled original of up to 8 megapixels is then compared with a 2x bicubic reading, kept only if the model prefers it and no confident word is lost. Every image with confident words gets a 1.5x Lanczos probe that can insert missing letters. Images with a side over 2000 pixels or text under 12 pixels are read again in 1024-pixel tiles, kept only if every earlier word is retained. The final OCR text goes to the injection check.
